dts: rk3576: armsom-sige5: reserve OP-TEE's secure DRAM

Rockchip's BL32 loads at the address in rkbin's RKTRUST/RK3576TRUST.ini and
publishes no memory reservation, so Linux is free to allocate over the secure
world. Reserve it.

Only apply this together with a bootloader built WITH_BL32=1. A kernel that
reserves DRAM for a secure world that is not there just loses the memory, and
a BL32 without the reservation crashes the board.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3576-armsom-sige5.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3576-armsom-sige5.dts
@@ -1221,3 +1221,30 @@
 		};
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * OP-TEE's secure DRAM. Rockchip's BL32 loads at the address given by
+ * [BL32_OPTION] in rkbin's RKTRUST/RK3576TRUST.ini and publishes no
+ * reservation of its own, so without this Linux allocates straight over the
+ * secure world and the board dies the moment either one touches it.
+ *
+ * Only meaningful with a BL32 in the boot image (WITH_BL32=1, see
+ * scripts/build-uboot.sh). Harmless without one, beyond losing the memory.
+ *
+ * The 32 MB size is a conservative guess taken from what OP-TEE reserves for
+ * TZDRAM elsewhere; confirm it against OP-TEE's own boot log before trusting
+ * it. Do not take the address from upstream optee_os: its rk3576 flavor puts
+ * TZDRAM at 0x70000000, which has nothing to do with where this blob loads.
+ */
+/ {
+	reserved-memory {
+		#address-cells = <2>;
+		#size-cells = <2>;
+		ranges;
+
+		optee@8400000 {
+			reg = <0x0 0x08400000 0x0 0x02000000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+	};
+};
